    "body": "In an Entity-Relationship (ER) model, suppose R is a many-to-one relationship from entity set E1 to entity set E2. Assume that E1 and E2 participate totally in R and that the cardinality of E1 is greater than the cardinality of E2.\n\nWhich one of the following is true about R?\n\n(a) Every entity in E1 is associated with exactly one\nentity in E2.\n\n(b) Some entity in E1 is associated with more than\none entity in E2.\n\n(c) Every entity in E2 is associated with exactly one\nentity in E1.\n\n(d) Every entity in E2 is associated with at most one\nentity in E1.\n\nER Model Concept and Conversion of ER Model to RDBMS",
    "subject": "DBMS",
    "chapter": "Database Design and ER Model",
    "day": 87,
    "answerKey": "a",
    "solution": "Cardinality of E1 > Cardinality of E2\n\n(a) Correct. Every entity of E1 is associated with\nexactly one entity in E2.\n\n(Θ its total participation and many to one from E1 to E2).\n\n(b) Incorrect. Because mention some entity in E1\nbut here total participation and mention more than one entity in E2 but here many to one (of E2 side).\n\n(c) Incorrect. Because there is a many to one from\nE1 to E2, not from E2 to E1.\n\n(d) Incorrect. Because there is a many to one from\nE1 to E2.",
